## Plot 1 - Lake Huron

The Lake Huron dataset contains water level data for Lake Huron, measured in feet, from NOAA.

Sometimes we get time series data in a format we recognize:

```{r}
tidyLakeHuron <- tibble(LakeHuron, Year = c(1875:1972))
tidyLakeHuron
```

And sometimes we get it in a specific ts format that is unique to time series data:

```{r}
class(LakeHuron_ts) 

```

## Task 1

If you want to graph a ts object directly, you can do it with base R pretty quickly and easily.

```{r}
plot(LakeHuron_ts)
```

It's a little harder to do with ggplot, but not so bad if you converted the data to make it tidy like above. I've made a very, very bad graph of the Lake Huron data with ggplot. Fix it so it more appropriately shows the time series with a line graph.

```{r}
ggplot(tidyLakeHuron) + aes(x = LakeHuron, y = Year) + geom_count()
```

It's more annoying to do with ggplot.

```{r}
ggplot(tidyLakeHuron) + aes(x = LakeHuron, y = Year) + geom_line()
```

